jerry_cust: Hey team — running into 401s from the browser after we deploy to Vercel Edge. Local Node dev works fine. In the network tab the fetch shows an "opaque" response and the app logs show a 401 with an empty body. Repro steps + minimal code below. Any pointers?

jerry_cust: package.json deps: `
{
  "next": "13.4.4",
  "redwood-inference-js": "0.3.2"
}
`

jerry_cust: Next API route (runtime: edge):
```js
export const runtime = 'edge';
import { createClient } from 'redwood-inference-js';
const client = createClient({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY });
export default async function handler(req) {
  const out = await client.generate({ prompt: 'hello' });
  return new Response(JSON.stringify(out));
}
```

jerry_cust: Dev console: `Error: 401` — network panel shows response type: "opaque" and preflight returns 204. I tried adding CORS headers in the route but no luck. Repro repo: https://github.com/jerry/repro-edge-sdk (private, DM me for access).

sara_support: Thanks for the repro steps. Can you confirm which SDK entrypoint you're importing (edge vs node/browser build) and whether you bundle any polyfills (node-fetch) into the Next build? Also please paste exact request headers from the network tab.

jerry_cust: I didn't intentionally add polyfills. Import is just `redwood-inference-js` (default). Headers from the request: `Origin: https://myapp.vercel.app`, `Authorization: Bearer ***masked***`, `Content-Type: application/json`. Preflight shows `Access-Control-Allow-Methods: GET,POST,OPTIONS` but no `Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Authorization`.

alex_devex: Quick suspicion: default package entry might be the Node-target build which expects a different fetch/headers behavior. For Vercel Edge you should import the edge-compatible bundle if we publish one. Workarounds: a) import the explicit edge entrypoint if available, e.g. `import { createClient } from 'redwood-inference-js/edge'` b) make sure the runtime doesn't polyfill node globals (no node-fetch). Also ensure the client is sending the Authorization header in the actual fetch (Edge runtimes sometimes strip credentials on opaque responses).

alex_devex: Example minimal edge-compatible handler to try: 
```js
export const runtime = 'edge';
import { createClient } from 'redwood-inference-js/edge';
const client = createClient({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY });
export default async function handler(req) {
  // confirm key present at runtime (mask in logs)
  console.log('rw_key_mask', process.env.RW_KEY ? process.env.RW_KEY.slice(-8) : 'undefined');
  const res = await client.generate({ prompt: 'hi' });
  return new Response(JSON.stringify(res), {
    status: 200,
    headers: {
      'Access-Control-Allow-Origin': req.headers.get('origin') || '*',
      'Access-Control-Allow-Credentials': 'true',
      'Access-Control-Allow-Headers': 'Authorization,Content-Type'
    }
  });
}
```

luis_engineer: To add: opaque responses often mean the browser blocked reading the response due to CORS/credentials. If your preflight doesn't explicitly allow `Authorization`, the browser won't include that header on the real request (or will treat the response as opaque). Confirm your upstream (the API we call) allows the Authorization header in CORS. If you're proxying through Vercel Edge, you also need to ensure the edge function forwards headers and doesn't drop Authorization on redirects.

luis_engineer: Also double-check env at runtime — Next + Vercel can inline env at build time; Edge functions still need the env set in the Vercel dashboard for the correct scope (Preview vs Production).

jerry_cust: Good catch — I added the Access-Control-Allow-Headers and switched to the `/edge` import. Still getting 401 but logs now show `rw_key_mask undefined`. I think my RW_KEY wasn't set for the production environment.

sara_support: That would explain it — 401 + empty body is consistent with the SDK sending a request with no auth header (server rejecting). Please set the env var in Vercel for the right scope and redeploy. If you can't expose the key in logs, mask it and only output the last 6 chars as above.

jerry_cust: Set the env in Vercel (Production) and redeployed — now requests return 200 and the app works again. Thanks all! :tada:

